this is what's left of tobruk's house of  representatives building  protesters in the eastern libyan city  stormed it overnight  looting its offices assembly hall and  setting it on fire  this protest came after the political  deadlock and worsening living conditions  in the capital it is clear that the  legislative houses won't come to an  agreement so this protest could pressure  the un mission and the international  community to turn to alternative  solutions  earlier on friday hundreds of young  protesters gathered at this square in  the capital tripoli  living conditions have deteriorated in  recent months after a parallel  government was appointed by the house of  representatives in eastern libya  with power cuts lasting up to 18 hours a  day  there's anger against political leaders  our demands include the removal of  injustice on the citizens  solving the problem of electricity  dissolving and dismantling all  legislative and executive bodies  such as the baba's government  bashaga's government the high state  council parliament aguila salah  2.8 million libyans registered to vote  in elections scheduled for last december  but they were postponed after libya's  two rival parliaments failed to agree on  the rules governing the poll  the disagreement is primarily about who  can run for president the most  controversial candidate is libyan  american warlord khalifa hafter who  controls the oil-rich eastern region  recent u.n mediated talks between the  two sides in cairo and geneva have  failed to achieve consensus  the delegation of the house of  representatives demanded to remove the  condition that presidential candidates  cannot hold any other nationality of  course we categorically rejected this  matter because in this case we are not  putting in general rules but rather it  may be interpreted as tailoring the  rules to allow certain people to run  while the protests got violent in tuberc  and elsewhere they remained largely  peaceful in tripoli the u.n support  mission here in the capital has urged  for calm and restraint what's clear for  now is people are frustrated with those  in power and want elections to be held  if there's no political breakthrough  soon the situation could get worse  malatrina al-jazeera tripoli
 

